# 29. RESPONSIVE DESIGN

The player interface must work properly on:

Mobile phones
Tablets
Laptops
Desktop monitors

Prioritize mobile landscape and portrait usability because teams may participate using phones.

Code blocks should remain readable.

Keypads and path selections must have large touch targets.

Do not create tiny controls.

Admin dashboard should prioritize laptop/desktop.

---

# 30. ANIMATIONS

Use Framer Motion.

Animations should enhance game feedback rather than slow gameplay.

Implement:

Door unlock
Gate opening
Map movement
Terminal typing
Success pulse
Incorrect answer shake
Vault opening
Fragment collection
Leaderboard position change
Final treasure reveal

Keep most animations between approximately 200–800ms.

Allow longer cinematic animation only for major transitions such as the final treasure.

Respect prefers-reduced-motion.

---

# 31. AUDIO

Create optional sound support.

Sounds:

button interaction
terminal typing
incorrect attempt
correct answer
door unlock
fragment discovery
vault opening
final victory

Audio must be muted by default until browser interaction permits playback.

Provide:

🔊 / 🔇

Do not make audio essential to solving any challenge.

---

# 32. LOADING AND NETWORK FAILURE

Because this may run over college Wi-Fi/LAN, handle unstable connections gracefully.

Never lose a team's completed progress because the browser refreshes.

Progress is server-side.

If network drops:

CONNECTION LOST

"Reconnecting to the expedition server..."

Automatically retry.

When connection returns:

CONNECTION RESTORED

Synchronize current progress from backend.

Prevent accidental duplicate scoring from repeated requests.

Use idempotent completion logic.

---

# 33. REFRESH / CHEATING PROTECTION

Refreshing must not:

reset timer
restore lives
restore hints
remove penalties
allow repeated completion points
skip levels

The backend is authoritative.

A team should only access quests it has legitimately unlocked unless an admin overrides progression.

---

