# 45. LAN MODE

This feature is important.

The organizer should be able to start the backend and frontend on one laptop.

Example concept:

Organizer laptop:
192.168.x.x

Participants connect through browser:

[http://192.168.x.x:PORT](http://192.168.x.x:PORT)

Configure the server to bind appropriately for LAN access.

Do not hardcode localhost into client requests in a way that prevents other devices from connecting.

Document firewall considerations.

Display a convenient "Participant URL" in the admin dashboard when possible.

Optionally provide a QR code representing the participant URL.

---

# 46. GAME MASTER EXPERIENCE

The organizer must be able to understand the entire event state at a glance.

Mission Control should prioritize:

EVENT STATUS

ACTIVE TEAMS

TEAMS STUCK > X MINUTES

CURRENT LEADER

COMPLETED TEAMS

SYSTEM CONNECTION STATUS

If a team remains on one quest unusually long, visually flag it:

POSSIBLY STUCK

This helps organizers decide whether to provide assistance.

---

# 47. DO NOT BUILD THESE PATTERNS

Do not create:

Generic Bootstrap admin-looking player interface.

Normal quiz cards.

Question 1/10 progress bars.

Radio-button MCQs for every level.

Large amounts of explanatory text.

Excessive neon.

Matrix rain.

Fake hacker terminal clichés everywhere.

Unnecessary 3D assets.

Heavy WebGL that performs poorly on phones.

Client-side answer checking.

Client-side authoritative scoring.

Arbitrary code execution without isolation.

Hardcoded progression scattered across frontend components.

---

# 48. DESIGN PHILOSOPHY

Every screen should answer:

"What is the player trying to discover?"

rather than:

"What question are they answering?"

Programming is the puzzle mechanism.

Exploration is the experience.

Progression is the reward.

The application should make a beginner feel curious rather than intimidated.

---

# 49. IMPLEMENTATION ORDER

Do not attempt everything simultaneously.

Implement in this order:

PHASE 1
Project architecture
Database
Authentication/session system
Quest model
Progression engine

PHASE 2
Player shell
HUD
Game map
Quest rendering engine

PHASE 3
Six quests
Answer validation
Scoring
Lives
Hints
Fragments

PHASE 4
Admin Mission Control
Team management
Event controls
Quest management

PHASE 5
Leaderboard
WebSockets
Realtime event state

PHASE 6
Animations
Audio
Environmental polish

PHASE 7
Physical treasure mode
QR/token verification

PHASE 8
Security review
Tests
Responsive QA
LAN testing
README
Production configuration

After each phase:

Run the application.
Fix compilation errors.
Fix runtime errors.
Test the implemented workflow.
Only then proceed to the next phase.

---

# 50. FIRST TASK

Begin by:

1. Analyze this specification.
2. Produce the final architecture.
3. Define database models.
4. Define API routes.
5. Define frontend route structure.
6. Define the game-state/progression state machine.
7. Create the project directory structure.
8. Initialize frontend and backend.
9. Implement Phase 1.
10. Run and test Phase 1 before moving forward.

Do not reduce this project to a prototype or static mockup.

Build an actual working full-stack Code Hunt platform.

When a design decision is unspecified, choose the simplest robust solution appropriate for a college event running on approximately 10–50 concurrent team devices.

Prioritize, in this order:

1. Reliable game progression
2. Server-authoritative security
3. Easy event administration
4. Mobile usability
5. Game atmosphere
6. Visual effects

The final result should feel like a real Programming Club game called CODE HUNT — not an online examination with a dark theme.
